## Step 4 — Reflection

The Nuclear Safety Engineer career path feels more aligned with my POV Statement because I am interested in problem solving and protecting people. My current Cybersecurity degree would prepare me well for a career as an Information Security Analyst but after I researched jobs like that I realized I am more interested in the engineering and scientific side of technology. I was sort of surprised by the fact that nuclear engineering uses a lot of the same skills that I am interested in like computer programming, math, and systems analysis. I also found it kind of cool how nuclear engineers can work in a bunch of different areas such as reactor design, nuclear safety, research, and nuclear energy, all of which I would be interested in. Completing the steps makes me want to commit to my decision to explore switching from cybersecurity to nuclear engineering because the coursework and career options seem to better match my interests. At the same time, a cybersecurity background could still be useful in nuclear engineering because nuclear facilities rely heavily on computer systems and cybersecurity. This leads me to wonder if having experience in both fields would make me more competitive for jobs in the nuclear engineering industry and for positions as a nuclear safety engineer. I need to delve deeper and research about this more to figure out how I want to dedicate my time in the future.
